(* Content-type: application/vnd.wolfram.mathematica *)
(* Bakry??mery Curvature - Computational Classnotes - CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 *)

Notebook[{
Cell["Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery Curvature", "Title"],
Cell["The Bochner formula without derivatives: curvature from the Laplacian alone \:2014 Curvature from the Laplacian, notebook 9", "Subtitle"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell["This notebook builds Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery curvature from the Laplacian alone. It starts from Bochner\:2019s formula on the round sphere, turns it into the \:0393-calculus, defines the curvature-dimension condition CD(K, N) on graphs, works the smallest cases by hand, computes exact values on families of graphs, and checks the Lichnerowicz eigenvalue bound and the Lin\:2013Yau dimension bound. Evaluate top to bottom; every section ends with a check. The write-up \:201cBakry\:2013\:00c9mery Curvature\:201d carries the same section numbers.", "Text"],
Cell["1. Toolkit", "Section"],
Cell["\:0394g[G, f] is the normalised graph Laplacian \:0394f(v) = mean over the neighbours w of f(w) \:2212 f(v). \:0393q is the carr\:00e9 du champ \:0393(f, g) = \:00bd(\:0394(fg) \:2212 f\:0394g \:2212 g\:0394f), which on a graph is \:00bd mean (f(w) \:2212 f(v))(g(w) \:2212 g(v)). \:03932q is \:0393\:2082(f) = \:00bd\:0394\:0393(f) \:2212 \:0393(f, \:0394f). At a vertex x both \:0393(f)(x) and \:0393\:2082(f)(x) are quadratic forms in the values of f on the 2-ball of x; beForms returns their matrices (with f(x) = 0, since constants drop out). KN[G, x, 1/N] is the exact largest K with \:0393\:2082 \:2212 (\:0394f)\:00b2/N \:2265 K\:0393 at x: the distance-2 values are eliminated by a Schur complement, then K is the smallest generalised eigenvalue against \:0393. KBE is the N = \:221e case.", "Text"],
Cell["ClearAll[nbr, \:0394g, \:0393q, \:03932q, beForms, KN, KBE, \:03baColour, \:03baLegend];\nnbr[G_, v_] := AdjacencyList[G, v];\n\:0394g[G_, f_][v_] := Mean[(f[#] - f[v]) & /@ nbr[G, v]];\n\:0393q[G_, f_, g_][v_] := 1/2 Mean[(f[#] - f[v]) (g[#] - g[v]) & /@ nbr[G, v]];\n\:03932q[G_, f_][x_] := 1/2 \:0394g[G, Association[# -> \:0393q[G, f, f][#] & /@ VertexList[G]]][x] -\n   \:0393q[G, f, Association[# -> \:0394g[G, f][#] & /@ VertexList[G]]][x];\nbeForms[G_, x_] := Module[{S1 = nbr[G, x], S2, vars, F, f, q1, q2, qD},\n  S2 = Complement[VertexList[NeighborhoodGraph[G, x, 2]], S1, {x}];\n  vars = Array[F, Length[S1] + Length[S2]];\n  f = Association[Join[Thread[VertexList[G] -> 0], Thread[Join[S1, S2] -> vars]]];\n  q1 = \:0393q[G, f, f][x]; q2 = \:03932q[G, f][x]; qD = \:0394g[G, f][x]^2;\n  Join[{Length[S1]}, (1/2 D[#, {vars, 2}]) & /@ {q1, q2, qD}]];\nKN[G_, x_, Ninv_] := Module[{k, M1, M2, MD, A, a, B, C, S},\n  {k, M1, M2, MD} = beForms[G, x]; A = M2 - Ninv MD;\n  a = A[[;; k, ;; k]]; B = A[[;; k, k + 1 ;;]]; C = A[[k + 1 ;;, k + 1 ;;]];\n  S = If[C === {}, a, a - B . Inverse[C] . Transpose[B]];\n  Min[Eigenvalues[{S, M1[[;; k, ;; k]]}]]];\nKBE[G_, x_] := KN[G, x, 0];\n\:03baColour[k_, s_] := With[{u = Clip[k/s, {-1, 1}]}, Blend[{RGBColor[0.10, 0.27, 0.75], GrayLevel[0.97], RGBColor[0.96, 0.47, 0.02]}, (1 + Sign[u] Abs[u]^0.55)/2]];\n\:03baLegend[s_, name_] := BarLegend[{\:03baColour[#, s] &, {-s, s}}, LegendLabel -> Style[name, 13, Black], LabelStyle -> {12, Black}];\ntree = KaryTree[15, 2];\ndumbbell = Graph[UndirectedEdge @@@ Join[List @@@ EdgeList[CompleteGraph[5]], (List @@@ EdgeList[CompleteGraph[5]]) + 7, {{5, 6}, {6, 7}, {7, 8}}]];\nfam = {{\"tree\", tree}, {\"C\:2086\", CycleGraph[6]}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5]}, {\"Petersen\", PetersenGraph[]}, {\"grid 4\:00d74\", GridGraph[{4, 4}]}, {\"dumbbell\", dumbbell}};", "Input"],
Cell["2. The carr\:00e9 du champ, and Bochner\:2019s formula on the sphere", "Section"],
Cell["Carr\:00e9 du champ (French: square of the field) is the operator that recovers |\:2207f|\:00b2 from the Laplacian alone. Since \:0394(f\:00b2) = 2f\:0394f + 2|\:2207f|\:00b2, define, for any Laplacian, \:0393(f, g) = \:00bd(\:0394(fg) \:2212 f\:0394g \:2212 g\:0394f) and \:0393(f) = \:0393(f, f). On a manifold \:0393(f, g) = \:27e8\:2207f, \:2207g\:27e9; on a graph \:0393(f)(v) = \:00bd mean (f(w) \:2212 f(v))\:00b2 over the neighbours w. The iterated carr\:00e9 du champ is \:0393\:2082(f) = \:00bd\:0394\:0393(f) \:2212 \:0393(f, \:0394f). The check below confirms the definition on the sphere (for any F), on the Petersen graph (symbolic values), and on the line for f = x\:00b2.", "Text"],
Cell["ClearAll[\:0394s, \:0393s];\n\:0394s[f_] := 1/Sin[\:03b8] D[Sin[\:03b8] D[f, \:03b8], \:03b8] + 1/Sin[\:03b8]^2 D[f, {\:03c6, 2}];\n\:0393s[f_, g_] := D[f, \:03b8] D[g, \:03b8] + 1/Sin[\:03b8]^2 D[f, \:03c6] D[g, \:03c6];\nWith[{G = PetersenGraph[], fa = Association[Thread[Range[10] -> Array[a, 10]]]},\n {Simplify[1/2 (\:0394s[F[\:03b8, \:03c6]^2] - 2 F[\:03b8, \:03c6] \:0394s[F[\:03b8, \:03c6]]) - \:0393s[F[\:03b8, \:03c6], F[\:03b8, \:03c6]]],\n  Simplify[1/2 (\:0394g[G, Map[#^2 &, fa]][1] - 2 fa[1] \:0394g[G, fa][1]) - \:0393q[G, fa, fa][1]],\n  With[{u = x^2}, Simplify[1/2 (D[u^2, {x, 2}] - 2 u D[u, {x, 2}])]]}]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: {0, 0, 4x\:00b2}. Bochner\:2019s formula follows.", "Text"],
Cell["On a Riemannian manifold, \:00bd\:0394|\:2207f|\:00b2 = \:27e8\:2207f, \:2207\:0394f\:27e9 + |Hess f|\:00b2 + Ric(\:2207f, \:2207f). In \:0393-notation, with \:0393(f) = |\:2207f|\:00b2 and \:0393\:2082(f) = \:00bd\:0394\:0393(f) \:2212 \:0393(f, \:0394f), this reads \:0393\:2082(f) = |Hess f|\:00b2 + Ric(\:2207f, \:2207f). On the unit sphere S\:00b2, Ric = 1, so \:0393\:2082 \:2212 \:0393 = |Hess f|\:00b2. The check below proves it for an arbitrary function F(\:03b8, \:03c6).", "Text"],
Cell["ClearAll[\:0394s, \:0393s, \:03932s, hess, hn2];\n\:0394s[f_] := 1/Sin[\:03b8] D[Sin[\:03b8] D[f, \:03b8], \:03b8] + 1/Sin[\:03b8]^2 D[f, {\:03c6, 2}];\n\:0393s[f_, g_] := D[f, \:03b8] D[g, \:03b8] + 1/Sin[\:03b8]^2 D[f, \:03c6] D[g, \:03c6];\n\:03932s[f_] := 1/2 \:0394s[\:0393s[f, f]] - \:0393s[f, \:0394s[f]];\nhess[f_] := {{D[f, {\:03b8, 2}], D[f, \:03b8, \:03c6] - Cot[\:03b8] D[f, \:03c6]}, {D[f, \:03b8, \:03c6] - Cot[\:03b8] D[f, \:03c6], D[f, {\:03c6, 2}] + Sin[\:03b8] Cos[\:03b8] D[f, \:03b8]}};\nhn2[f_] := With[{H = hess[f]}, H[[1, 1]]^2 + 2 H[[1, 2]]^2/Sin[\:03b8]^2 + H[[2, 2]]^2/Sin[\:03b8]^4];\nSimplify[\:03932s[F[\:03b8, \:03c6]] - \:0393s[F[\:03b8, \:03c6], F[\:03b8, \:03c6]] - hn2[F[\:03b8, \:03c6]]]", "Input"],
Cell["Since |Hess f|\:00b2 \:2265 (\:0394f)\:00b2/2 in dimension 2 (Cauchy\:2013Schwarz on the trace), \:0393\:2082 \:2265 \:0393 + (\:0394f)\:00b2/2: the sphere satisfies CD(1, 2). Equality needs a pure-trace Hessian. The height function z = cos \:03b8 is the first eigenfunction (\:0394z = \:22122z) and attains it everywhere.", "Text"],
Cell["{Simplify[{\:03932s[Cos[\:03b8]], \:0393s[Cos[\:03b8], Cos[\:03b8]], \:0394s[Cos[\:03b8]]^2/2}], Simplify[\:03932s[Cos[\:03b8]] - \:0393s[Cos[\:03b8], Cos[\:03b8]] - \:0394s[Cos[\:03b8]]^2/2]}", "Input"],
Cell["For g = z + (x\:00b2 \:2212 y\:00b2) = cos \:03b8 + sin\:00b2\:03b8 cos 2\:03c6, which mixes the first two eigenspaces, the slack \:0393\:2082 \:2212 \:0393 \:2212 (\:0394g)\:00b2/2 is 8 \:2212 8 sin\:00b2\:03b8 + sin\:2074\:03b8 (1 + cos 4\:03c6), so it vanishes only where sin \:03b8 = 1 and cos 4\:03c6 = \:22121: four points of the equator. Fig. 1 shows it.", "Text"],
Cell["gS = Cos[\:03b8] + Sin[\:03b8]^2 Cos[2 \:03c6];\nslack = FullSimplify[\:03932s[gS] - \:0393s[gS, gS] - \:0394s[gS]^2/2];\n{slack, MinMax[Table[slack, {\:03b8, 0.01, Pi - 0.01, Pi/60}, {\:03c6, 0, 2 Pi, Pi/60}]], Simplify[slack /. \:03b8 -> Pi/2]}", "Input"],
Cell["slackCol[v_] := Blend[{GrayLevel[0.98], RGBColor[1., 0.62, 0.20], RGBColor[0.92, 0.38, 0.], RGBColor[0.50, 0.20, 0.]}, (v/8)^0.8];\npic = Show[SphericalPlot3D[1, {\:03b8, 0, Pi}, {\:03c6, 0, 2 Pi}, ColorFunction -> Function[{x, y, z, t, p, r}, slackCol[slack /. {\:03b8 -> t, \:03c6 -> p}]],\n  ColorFunctionScaling -> False, Mesh -> None, PlotPoints -> 70, Lighting -> {{\"Ambient\", GrayLevel[0.82]}, {\"Directional\", GrayLevel[0.28], ImageScaled[{2, 2, 3}]}}],\n Graphics3D[{GrayLevel[0.25], Thickness[0.004], Line[Table[1.004 {Cos[p], Sin[p], 0}, {p, 0, 2 Pi, Pi/90}]], Black, PointSize[0.03], Point[Table[1.01 {Cos[p], Sin[p], 0}, {p, Pi/4, 7 Pi/4, Pi/2}]]}],\n Boxed -> False, Axes -> False, SphericalRegion -> True, ViewPoint -> {2.4, 1.0, 0.9}, ImageSize -> 340];\nRow[{pic, BarLegend[{slackCol, {0, 8}}, LegendLabel -> Style[\"slack\", 12, Black], LabelStyle -> {12, Black}]}]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 1. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "The slack \:0393\:2082 \:2212 \:0393 \:2212 (\:0394g)\:00b2/2 of CD(1, 2) on the unit sphere for g = z + (x\:00b2 \:2212 y\:00b2): zero only at the four equator points marked in black, largest, 8, at the poles."}], "Text"],
Cell["Check: the identity \:0393\:2082 \:2212 \:0393 \:2212 |Hess F|\:00b2 simplifies to 0 for arbitrary F; for z the slack is exactly 0; for g the slack lies in [0, 8].", "Text"],
Cell["3. Definition on graphs", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"On a graph \:0393 and \:0393\:2082 need only the Laplacian, so the Bochner inequality can be imposed directly. A vertex x satisfies CD(K, N) if \:0393\:2082(f)(x) \:2265 (1/N)(\:0394f(x))\:00b2 + K \:0393(f)(x) for every function f. The Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery curvature ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], "(x) is the largest such K; K\:221e(x) drops the dimension term. \:0393(f)(x) depends on the 1-ball of x and \:0393\:2082(f)(x) on the 2-ball, so ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], "(x) is a finite eigenvalue problem."}], "Text"],
Cell["Dataset@Association[Function[g, g[[1]] -> N@Min[KBE[g[[2]], #] & /@ VertexList[g[[2]]]]] /@ fam]", "Input"],
Cell["Check (minimum over vertices): tree \:22121/3, C\:2086 0, Q\:2083 2/3, K\:2085 7/8, Petersen \:22121/3, 4\:00d74 grid 0.0876 (an algebraic number), dumbbell \:22120.383 = (2 \:2212 \:221a34)/10.", "Text"],
Cell["4. Worked by hand", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"One edge K\:2082. Put f(x) = 0, f(y) = t. Then \:0394f(x) = t, \:0394f(y) = \:2212t, and \:0393(f) = t\:00b2/2 at both ends, so \:0394\:0393(f)(x) = 0. \:0393(f, \:0394f)(x) = \:00bd(t \:2212 0)(\:2212t \:2212 t) = \:2212t\:00b2, hence \:0393\:2082(f)(x) = 0 \:2212 (\:2212t\:00b2) = t\:00b2. So \:0393\:2082/\:0393 = 2: K\:221e = 2. With the dimension term, t\:00b2 \:2212 t\:00b2/N \:2265 K t\:00b2/2 gives ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], " = 2 \:2212 2/N."}], "Text"],
Cell["{KBE[CompleteGraph[2], 1], Table[KN[CompleteGraph[2], 1, 1/n], {n, {1, 2, 4, 10}}]}", "Input"],
Cell["Long cycles are flat. On C\:2099 with n \:2265 5, take f(v) = position along the path through x (values \:22122, \:22121, 0, 1, 2 on the 2-ball). Then \:0394f = 0 near x, and \:0393(f) = \:00bd at every vertex, so \:0394\:0393 = 0 and \:0393\:2082(f)(x) = 0 while \:0393(f)(x) = \:00bd. Hence K\:221e \:2264 0; the kernel gives exactly 0.", "Text"],
Cell["Table[KBE[CycleGraph[n], 1], {n, 3, 9}]", "Input"],
Cell["Complete graphs. On K\:2099 every other vertex is a neighbour, so there is no 2-ball part. The same calculation, with \:0394f(x) = mean of the neighbour values, gives K\:221e(K\:2099) = (n + 2)/(2(n \:2212 1)): 2 for K\:2082, 5/4 for K\:2083, 7/8 for K\:2085, falling to \:00bd as n \:2192 \:221e.", "Text"],
Cell["Table[{n, KBE[CompleteGraph[n], 1], (n + 2)/(2 (n - 1))}, {n, 2, 8}]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{"Check: K\:2082 gives 2 and ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], " = 2 \:2212 2/N; C\:2083 = K\:2083 gives 5/4, C\:2084 = Q\:2082 gives 1, C\:2099 (n \:2265 5) gives 0; K\:2099 matches (n + 2)/(2(n \:2212 1)) for n = 2 \:2026 8."}], "Text"],
Cell["5. Families", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"The hypercube ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Q", "d"]]], " has K\:221e = 2/d at every vertex. A tree is negatively curved at the root and branch vertices and positive at the leaves (2/3). On the dumbbell the two bridge vertices are the most negative, (2 \:2212 \:221a34)/10 \:2248 \:22120.383; the vertex where a clique meets the bridge is just below zero, (35 \:2212 \:221a1385)/80 \:2248 \:22120.028."}], "Text"],
Cell["{Table[{d, KBE[HypercubeGraph[d], 1]}, {d, 1, 5}], Table[KBE[tree, v], {v, {1, 2, 4, 8}}], Table[KBE[dumbbell, v], {v, {1, 5, 6, 7}}]}", "Input"],
Cell["Fig. 2 colours every vertex of the seven example graphs by K\:221e, on the same blue\:2013white\:2013orange scale as the Ollivier gallery.", "Text"],
Cell["vertexPlot[G_, label_] := With[{ks = KBE[G, #] & /@ VertexList[G]},\n  Graph[G, VertexStyle -> Thread[VertexList[G] -> (Directive[EdgeForm[{GrayLevel[0.1], Thickness[0.004]}], \:03baColour[#, 1.25]] & /@ ks)],\n   VertexSize -> If[VertexCount[G] > 12, 0.32, 0.22], EdgeStyle -> Directive[GrayLevel[0.35], Thickness[0.006]], ImageSize -> 190,\n   PlotLabel -> Style[Row[{label, \"  min \", With[{k = Min[ks]}, If[MatchQ[k, _Integer | _Rational], k, NumberForm[N[k], 3]]]}], 11, GrayLevel[0.1]], ImagePadding -> {{6, 6}, {6, 34}}]];\nLegended[Grid[Partition[vertexPlot[#[[2]], #[[1]]] & /@ fam, 4, 4, {1, 1}, \"\"]], \:03baLegend[1.25, Subscript[\"K\", \"\:221e\"]]]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 2. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], "Seven graphs with every vertex coloured by its Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery curvature K\:221e. Complete graphs and cubes are positive, long cycles flat; tree branch points and the dumbbell\:2019s bridge are negative."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Check: K\:221e(", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Q", "d"]]], ") = 2/d for d = 1 \:2026 5; tree: root \:22121/3, second level \:22121/3, third level (5 \:2212 \:221a33)/6 \:2248 \:22120.124, leaf 2/3; dumbbell: clique vertex (93 \:2212 \:221a793)/80 \:2248 0.81, attaching vertex \:2248 \:22120.028, bridge vertices \:2248 \:22120.383."}], "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"6. Dimension: the curve ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]]}], "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"Adding the dimension term lowers the curvature: ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], "(x) increases with N and tends to K\:221e(x). Fig. 3 plots N \:21a6 ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], " for four vertex-transitive graphs (every vertex has the same value). On C\:2086 and Petersen ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], " already equals K\:221e at N = 2: the function that attains K\:221e there has \:0394f(x) = 0, so the dimension term costs nothing. On K\:2085 and Q\:2083 ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], " only approaches K\:221e. Lin and Yau proved that every graph satisfies CD(1/D \:2212 1, 2), where D is the maximum degree; the check confirms K\:2082 \:2265 1/D \:2212 1 at every vertex."}], "Text"],
Cell["curves = Table[{g[[1]], Table[{n, KN[g[[2]], 1, 1/n]}, {n, 1, 30, 0.5}]},\n   {g, {{\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5]}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\"C\:2086\", CycleGraph[6]}, {\"Petersen\", PetersenGraph[]}}}];\nListLinePlot[curves[[All, 2]], PlotLegends -> curves[[All, 1]], AxesLabel -> {\"N\", Subscript[\"K\", \"N\"]},\n PlotStyle -> (Directive[#, AbsoluteThickness[3]] & /@ {RGBColor[0.90, 0.42, 0.], RGBColor[0.10, 0.27, 0.75], RGBColor[0., 0.50, 0.45], RGBColor[0.50, 0.20, 0.72]}),\n BaseStyle -> {FontSize -> 13, FontColor -> GrayLevel[0.1]}, AxesStyle -> Directive[GrayLevel[0.2], AbsoluteThickness[1.2]],\n Epilog -> {Dashing[{0.02, 0.012}], AbsoluteThickness[1.8], RGBColor[0.90, 0.42, 0.], Line[{{0, 7/8}, {30, 7/8}}], RGBColor[0.10, 0.27, 0.75], Line[{{0, 2/3}, {30, 2/3}}]},\n PlotRange -> {{0, 30}, {-1.4, 1}}, PlotLabel -> \"Curvature with a dimension term rises to K\:221e (dashed)\", ImageSize -> 520, ImagePadding -> {{46, 30}, {34, 34}}, GridLines -> {None, {0}}]", "Input"],
Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["Fig. 3. ", FontWeight -> "Bold", FontColor -> RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11]], Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], " against N on K\:2085, Q\:2083, C\:2086 and Petersen. Each curve rises to its K\:221e (7/8 and 2/3 dashed; 0 and \:22121/3 are reached at N = 2)."}], "Text"],
Cell["AllTrue[fam, Function[g, With[{D = Max[VertexDegree[g[[2]]]]}, AllTrue[VertexList[g[[2]]], KN[g[[2]], #, 1/2] >= 1/D - 1 &]]]]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: the curves increase in N; K\:2082 \:2265 1/D \:2212 1 holds at every vertex of all seven graphs (True).", "Text"],
Cell["7. What a lower bound buys: Lichnerowicz and Bonnet\:2013Myers", "Section"],
Cell["If every vertex satisfies CD(K, N) with K > 0, the first non-zero eigenvalue of \:2212\:0394 satisfies \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 NK/(N \:2212 1); with N = \:221e, \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 K. The table compares \:03bb\:2081 with the best bound over N.", "Text"],
Cell["spec[G_] := With[{A = N[AdjacencyMatrix[G]], d = N[VertexDegree[G]]}, Sort[Eigenvalues[IdentityMatrix[Length[d]] - DiagonalMatrix[1/d] . A]][[2]]];\nDataset@Association[Function[g, g[[1]] -> With[{G = g[[2]]}, <|\"\:03bb\:2081\" -> spec[G], \"K\:221e\" -> N@Min[KBE[G, #] & /@ VertexList[G]],\n       \"best NK/(N\:22121)\" -> Max[Table[With[{k = Min[KN[G, #, 1/n] & /@ VertexList[G]]}, If[k > 0, N[n k/(n - 1)], 0]], {n, {2, 3, 4, 6, 10, 20, 50}}]]|>]] /@\n   {{\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5]}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\"K\:2083\", CompleteGraph[3]}, {\"Q\:2084\", HypercubeGraph[4]}}]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 K\:221e in every row, with equality on the cubes Q\:2083 (2/3) and Q\:2084 (1/2). On these graphs the N = \:221e bound is the strongest; the best finite-N bound is a little lower.", "Text"],
Cell[TextData[{"Diameter. Liu, M\:00fcnch and Peyerimhoff prove diam \:2264 2 ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Deg", "max"]]], "/K under CD(K, \:221e) for the unnormalised Laplacian, sharp on hypercubes. For a d-regular graph the normalised Laplacian is 1/d times the unnormalised one, so K\:221e scales by 1/d and the bound becomes diam \:2264 2/K\:221e."}], "Text"],
Cell["Grid[Prepend[Function[g, With[{k = Min[KBE[g[[2]], #] & /@ VertexList[g[[2]]]]}, {g[[1]], GraphDiameter[g[[2]]], k, 2/k}]] /@\n    {{\"K\:2083\", CompleteGraph[3]}, {\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5]}, {\"Q\:2082\", HypercubeGraph[2]}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3]}, {\"Q\:2084\", HypercubeGraph[4]}},\n   {\"graph\", \"diam\", Subscript[\"K\", \"\:221e\"], \"2/K\:221e\"}], Frame -> All]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: diam \:2264 2/K\:221e on every row, with equality on the cubes Q\:2082, Q\:2083, Q\:2084 (diameter d = 2/(2/d)).", "Text"],
Cell["8. Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery and Ollivier side by side", "Section"],
Cell["Both curvatures copy a property of Ricci curvature, Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery the Bochner formula and Ollivier the transport of balls. They agree in sign on these examples but not in size. The Ollivier values are from notebook 7 (Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau curvature, minimum over edges).", "Text"],
Cell["Grid[Prepend[Function[g, {g[[1]], N@Min[KBE[g[[2]], #] & /@ VertexList[g[[2]]]], g[[3]]}] /@\n    {{\"K\:2085\", CompleteGraph[5], \"5/4\"}, {\"Q\:2083\", HypercubeGraph[3], \"2/3\"}, {\"C\:2086\", CycleGraph[6], \"0\"}, {\"Petersen\", PetersenGraph[], \"0\"}, {\"grid 4\:00d74\", GridGraph[{4, 4}], \"0\"}},\n   {\"graph\", Subscript[\"K\", \"\:221e\"], Row[{\"min \", Subscript[\"\:03ba\", \"LLY\"]}]}], Frame -> All]", "Input"],
Cell["Check: on Q\:2083 both give 2/3; on K\:2085 Ollivier is larger (5/4 against 7/8); on Petersen Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery is negative (\:22121/3) while Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau is 0.", "Text"],
Cell["9. Exercises", "Section"],
Cell[TextData[{"1. Show by hand that K\:221e(K\:2083) = 5/4. 2. Show ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["K", "N"]]], "(K\:2082) = 2 \:2212 2/N from the K\:2082 calculation. 3. Find f on C\:2084 with \:0393\:2082(f) = \:0393(f) at a vertex, so K\:221e(C\:2084) \:2264 1. 4. Prove K\:221e(", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["Q", "d"]]], ") \:2264 2/d with f = one coordinate. 5. On S\:00b2, show that the slack of g is 8 \:2212 8 sin\:00b2\:03b8 + sin\:2074\:03b8 (1 + cos 4\:03c6) and find its four zeros."}], "Text"],
Cell["{KBE[CompleteGraph[3], 1], KBE[CycleGraph[4], 1], slack /. {\:03b8 -> Pi/2, \:03c6 -> Pi/4}}", "Input"],
Cell["Check: 5/4, 1, 0.", "Text"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"]
},
